{
  "case_id": "2019-19-518",
  "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Colorado Department of State",
      "case_id": "2019-19-518",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab96a9731462-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Constitution authorizes states to use their plenary authority to remove a bribed elector, one who engages in a rebellion, or one who would perpetrate a bait and switch on the people of their state by voting contrary to a binding pledge. By contrast, if a state wishes to treat electors as free agents, rather than as proxy voters, it is free to do so."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2019-19-518",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab96a9731462-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Would you state for me exactly what you think the limits on the state power to replace electors are?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2019-19-518",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab96a9731462-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. General, you start your brief questioning standing in this case. I wonder if you think, under our precedent, there's standing when a person is removed from an elected office?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-19-518",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab96a9731462-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Can you give us an idea of the practical consequences of a ruling one way or another? How would a ruling against you actually alter our democratic processes?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Colorado Department of State",
      "case_id": "2019-19-518",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab96a9731462-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. WEISER: Your Honor, the chaos that could result from upholding the Tenth Circuit's ruling is one that could occasion a constitutional crisis, as was noted by my colleague from Washington. If states have no ability to remove bribed electors and all that's left is Congress's ability to choose to count or not count, the mere fact of bribing electors in an open enough way would knock out electors, would limit who could vote, and ultimately could sway the outcome of a presidential election."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-19-518",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab96a9731462-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: And -- and if you're relying on the Qualifications Clause, couldn't you be said to be imposing a qualification too?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2019-19-518",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab96a9731462-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Counsel, I'd like to continue the same line of questioning that Justice Kagan raised with you and --and"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2019-19-518",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab96a9731462-0001-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF JASON HARROW ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENTS"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2019-19-518",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab96a9731462-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: The problem that I view is that then any two people, a plaintiff and defendant, who would like an issue decided by us, simply have to waive enough matters so that it has to come before us because it's not jurisdictional."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2019-19-518",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab96a9731462-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Yeah, I'll look -- I'll look at that. One other question. You didn't mention in terms of accountability what I take it -- why didn't you -- is that Congress doesn't have to count a vote of a faithless elector."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2019-19-518",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab96a9731462-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: What would you say if I said that if I think that there's silence, the best thing to do is leave it to the states, to not impose any constitutional requirement on them?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Philip J. Weiser",
      "case_id": "2019-19-518",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ab96a973146271a6db0f5ed3d603a671cced1f750a36e797646e4ce01ce15dff",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ab96a9731462-0002-0007",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "REBUTTAL ARGUMENT OF GEN. PHILIP J. WEISER ON BEHALF OF THE PETITIONER"
    },
